### Built-in modules

Two document models ship with the platform and are not registered for you — pass them to `withDocumentModelSources` whenever your reactor must handle document models or drives:

```typescript
import { documentModelDocumentModelModule } from "document-model";
import {
  driveDocumentModelModule,
  driveDocumentType, // "powerhouse/document-drive"
} from "@powerhousedao/shared/document-drive";

const reactor = await new ReactorBuilder()
  .withDocumentModelSources([
    documentModelDocumentModelModule, // powerhouse/document-model
    driveDocumentModelModule,         // powerhouse/document-drive
    ...myModules,
  ])
  .build();
```

Without `driveDocumentModelModule` the reactor cannot create or load drives, and `client.createEmpty(driveDocumentType)` fails to resolve a module.

### Creating a document with `IReactor.create`

`IReactor.create` takes a fully-formed `PHDocument`, so you build the document first with its model's creator and then hand it to the reactor. For the built-in drive model, both the module and the creator come from `@powerhousedao/shared/document-drive`:

```typescript
import { ReactorBuilder } from "@powerhousedao/reactor";
import { documentModelDocumentModelModule } from "document-model";
import {
  driveDocumentModelModule,
  driveCreateDocument,
} from "@powerhousedao/shared/document-drive";

const module = await new ReactorBuilder()
  // register every model you intend to write, including the built-ins
  .withDocumentModelSources([
    documentModelDocumentModelModule,
    driveDocumentModelModule,
  ])
  .buildModule();

const driveDoc = driveCreateDocument();
const job = await module.reactor.create(driveDoc);
```

Each document model package exports a `<model>CreateDocument()` helper alongside its `<model>DocumentModelModule`: `documentModelDocumentModelModule` and `documentModelCreateDocument` come from `document-model`, the drive equivalents from `@powerhousedao/shared/document-drive`. A `create` for a type whose module was not registered fails the job.

```typescript
import {
  ReactorEventTypes,
  SyncEventTypes,
  type JobReadReadyEvent,
  type SyncFailedEvent,
} from "@powerhousedao/reactor";

// `subscribe<K>` takes the payload type as an explicit type argument: the event
// id is a plain `number`, so TypeScript cannot infer the payload from it. Without
// the type argument `event` is `unknown` and `strict` builds fail with TS18046.
const unsubscribe = module.eventBus.subscribe<JobReadReadyEvent>(
  ReactorEventTypes.JOB_READ_READY,
  (type, event) => {
    console.log("Job completed:", event.jobId);
    console.log("Operations:", event.operations.length);
  },
);

// Listen for sync failures
module.eventBus.subscribe<SyncFailedEvent>(
  SyncEventTypes.SYNC_FAILED,
  (type, event) => {
    console.error("Sync failed for job:", event.jobId, event.errors);
  },
);
```

Payload types for the core job events are exported from `@powerhousedao/reactor`: `JOB_READ_READY` carries `JobReadReadyEvent` (`{ jobId: string; operations: OperationWithContext[] }`), `JOB_WRITE_READY` carries `JobWriteReadyEvent`, and `JOB_FAILED` carries `JobFailedEvent` (`{ jobId: string; error: Error; job?: Job }`).

### Registering the built-in document models

A reactor only understands document types whose modules it was given. Two built-in modules ship with the platform and are registered like any other source:

```typescript
import { ReactorBuilder } from "@powerhousedao/reactor";
import { documentModelDocumentModelModule } from "document-model";
import { driveDocumentModelModule } from "@powerhousedao/shared/document-drive";

const reactor = await new ReactorBuilder()
  .withDocumentModelSources([
    documentModelDocumentModelModule, // powerhouse/document-model
    driveDocumentModelModule,         // powerhouse/document-drive
    todoListModule,                   // your own models
  ])
  .build();
```

`documentModelDocumentModelModule` comes from `document-model` and lets the reactor store document-model definitions themselves. `driveDocumentModelModule` comes from the `@powerhousedao/shared/document-drive` subpath and is required before the reactor can create or mutate drives.

#### Building the `PHDocument` for `create`

`reactor.create` takes a fully formed `PHDocument`, not a type string. Every document-model module exports a matching `createDocument` factory of type `CreateDocument<TState> = (initialState?: Partial<TState>, createState?: CreateState<TState>) => PHDocument<TState>`; call it with no arguments to get an empty document of that type:

```typescript
import { driveCreateDocument } from "@powerhousedao/shared/document-drive";
import { documentModelCreateDocument } from "document-model";

const drive = driveCreateDocument();                 // powerhouse/document-drive
const job = await reactor.create(drive);

const modelDoc = documentModelCreateDocument();      // powerhouse/document-model
```

Codegen'd models export the same factory from their `utils` barrel (`utils.createDocument()`). `signer` is optional on `IReactor.create`; omit it when the reactor is not enforcing signatures.

`ReactorClientBuilder.buildModule()` returns an `InProcessReactorClientModule`. Alongside `client`, it exposes the in-process internals directly and non-optionally — `reactor`, `eventBus`, `documentIndexer`, `documentView`, `signer`, `subscriptionManager`, `jobAwaiter` — plus `reactorModule`, which is typed `InProcessReactorModule | undefined` (it is `undefined` for hosts whose reactor graph is not in-process). Prefer the top-level fields; if you need the full `reactorModule` graph, narrow it first:

```typescript
const { client, reactor, eventBus, reactorModule } = await new ReactorClientBuilder()
  .withReactorBuilder(builder)
  .buildModule();

if (!reactorModule) {
  throw new Error("Expected an in-process reactor module.");
}

const ops = await reactorModule.operationStore.getSince(doc.header.id, "global", "main", 0);
reactor.kill();
```

`IEventBus.subscribe` is generic in the event payload and does not infer it from the event-type constant, so pass the payload type explicitly. The reactor event payload types (`JobReadReadyEvent`, `JobWriteReadyEvent`, `JobFailedEvent`, `SyncFailedEvent`, …) are exported from `@powerhousedao/reactor`.

```typescript
import {
  ReactorEventTypes,
  SyncEventTypes,
  type JobReadReadyEvent,
  type SyncFailedEvent,
} from "@powerhousedao/reactor";

// Listen for all completed jobs
const unsubscribe = module.eventBus.subscribe<JobReadReadyEvent>(
  ReactorEventTypes.JOB_READ_READY,
  (type, event) => {
    console.log("Job completed:", event.jobId);
    console.log("Operations:", event.operations.length);
  },
);

// Listen for sync failures
module.eventBus.subscribe<SyncFailedEvent>(SyncEventTypes.SYNC_FAILED, (type, event) => {
  console.error("Sync failed for job:", event.jobId, event.errors);
});
```

Without the type argument the callback's `event` is `unknown` and any field access fails to compile.

### Registering the built-in document models

A reactor only knows the models you register. Two built-ins are needed by almost every host:

```typescript
import { ReactorBuilder } from "@powerhousedao/reactor";
import { documentModelDocumentModelModule } from "document-model";
import { driveDocumentModelModule } from "@powerhousedao/shared/document-drive";

const reactor = await new ReactorBuilder()
  .withDocumentModelSources([
    documentModelDocumentModelModule, // powerhouse/document-model
    driveDocumentModelModule,         // powerhouse/document-drive
    myDocumentModel,
  ])
  .build();
```

`driveDocumentModelModule` is required for anything under `client.drives` — without it `client.drives.create()` fails because the reactor cannot resolve `powerhouse/document-drive`.

Create a new drive document and wait for completion.

```typescript
create(
  input: DriveInput,
  signal?: AbortSignal,
): Promise<DocumentDriveDocument>
```

`DriveInput` is exported from `@powerhousedao/shared/document-drive`:

```typescript
type DriveInput = {
  global: { name: string; icon?: string | null };
  id?: string;
  slug?: string;
  preferredEditor?: string;
  local?: Partial<DocumentDriveLocalState>;
};
```

| Name | Type | Required | Description |
| ---- | ---- | -------- | ----------- |
| `global.name` | `string` | Yes | Drive name |
| `global.icon` | `string \| null` | No | Drive icon url |
| `id` | `string` | No | Explicit document id; generated when omitted |
| `slug` | `string` | No | Human-readable drive slug |
| `preferredEditor` | `string` | No | Editor id hosts should open the drive with |
| `signal` | `AbortSignal` | No | Cancel the request |

```typescript
const drive = await client.drives.create({ global: { name: "Demo Drive" } });
```

The drive model must be registered on the reactor (`driveDocumentModelModule`) for this call to succeed.

`ReactorClientBuilder.buildModule()` returns an `InProcessReactorClientModule`: the `client` plus non-optional direct references to the in-process internals — `reactor`, `eventBus`, `documentIndexer`, `documentView`, `signer`, `subscriptionManager` and `jobAwaiter`. It also carries `reactorModule`, but that field is typed `InProcessReactorModule | undefined` (the base `ReactorClientModule` contract allows hosts that have no in-process module), so under `strict` you must narrow it before use. Prefer the top-level fields:

```typescript
const { client, reactor, eventBus } = await new ReactorClientBuilder()
  .withReactorBuilder(builder)
  .buildModule();

const unsubscribe = eventBus.subscribe(ReactorEventTypes.JOB_READ_READY, () => {});
await reactor.kill();
```

If you need the full module (for `operationStore`, `processorManager`, …), narrow it first: `const { reactorModule } = module; if (!reactorModule) throw new Error("no in-process reactor module");`

### Registering the built-in document models

Two document models ship with the platform and must be registered explicitly — `ReactorBuilder` does not add them for you:

```typescript
import { documentModelDocumentModelModule } from "document-model";
import { driveDocumentModelModule } from "@powerhousedao/shared/document-drive";

const reactor = await new ReactorBuilder()
  .withDocumentModelSources([
    documentModelDocumentModelModule, // powerhouse/document-model
    driveDocumentModelModule, // powerhouse/document-drive
    ...yourModules,
  ])
  .build();
```

Register `driveDocumentModelModule` whenever the reactor will create or hold drives (`client.createEmpty("powerhouse/document-drive")`, `client.drives.addFile`); without it the drive type is unresolvable.

```ts
import { ReactorBuilder, ReactorEventTypes } from "@powerhousedao/reactor";
import { documentModelDocumentModelModule } from "document-model";
import { driveDocumentModelModule } from "@powerhousedao/shared/document-drive";
import { DocumentCountReadModel } from "./src/document-count-read-model.js";

const countReadModel = new DocumentCountReadModel();

const reactorModule = await new ReactorBuilder()
  .withDocumentModelSources([
    documentModelDocumentModelModule,
    driveDocumentModelModule,
  ])
  .withReadModel(countReadModel)
  .buildModule();

// After any job completes, counts are already up to date:
reactorModule.eventBus.subscribe(ReactorEventTypes.JOB_READ_READY, () => {
  console.log(countReadModel.getCounts());
});
```

### Registering the built-in document models

A reactor only knows the document models you give it. The two built-ins ship in separate packages:

```typescript
import { ReactorBuilder } from "@powerhousedao/reactor";
import { documentModelDocumentModelModule } from "document-model";
import {
  driveDocumentModelModule,
  driveCreateDocument,
} from "@powerhousedao/shared/document-drive";

const module = await new ReactorBuilder()
  .withDocumentModelSources([
    documentModelDocumentModelModule, // powerhouse/document-model
    driveDocumentModelModule,         // powerhouse/document-drive
  ])
  .buildModule();

// `driveCreateDocument()` returns an empty drive `PHDocument` you can pass to
// the low-level write API:
const job = await module.reactor.create(driveCreateDocument());
```

`@powerhousedao/shared` exposes these through the `./document-drive` subpath export (ESM only — there is no CJS `require` condition). Register `driveDocumentModelModule` whenever the reactor will hold drives; without it, creating a drive document fails at reducer lookup.

| `withJobAwaiter(awaiter)`           | Provide a custom job awaiter (see [Awaiting a job without a client](#awaiting-a-job-without-a-client)) |

### Awaiting a job without a client

`IReactor` write methods return a `PENDING` `JobInfo` immediately. On the low-level path there is no `waitForJob()` — construct a `JobAwaiter` over the module's event bus instead of polling `getJobStatus`:

```typescript
import { JobAwaiter } from "@powerhousedao/reactor";

const { reactor, eventBus } = module;
const awaiter = new JobAwaiter(eventBus, (jobId, signal) =>
  reactor.getJobStatus(jobId, signal),
);

const job = await reactor.create(document);
const info = await awaiter.waitForJob(job.id); // resolves at READ_READY, rejects on FAILED

awaiter.shutdown(); // synchronously rejects anything still pending
```

The awaiter subscribes to the job lifecycle events and reconciles events that arrived before `waitForJob` was called, so awaiting is race-free. Always call `shutdown()` before `reactor.kill()`.

### Creating the document to pass to `create`

`reactor.create` takes a fully-formed `PHDocument`, not a name or a type string. Each document model package exports a factory that builds an empty document of its type:

```typescript
import { documentModelCreateDocument, documentModelDocumentModelModule } from "document-model";
import { driveCreateDocument, driveDocumentModelModule } from "@powerhousedao/shared/document-drive";

// a drive (container) document
const drive = driveCreateDocument();
await reactor.create(drive);

// a regular document
const doc = documentModelCreateDocument();
await reactor.create(doc);
console.log(doc.header.id); // the id the reactor will use
```

The matching modules — `driveDocumentModelModule` and `documentModelDocumentModelModule` — must be registered on the builder with `withDocumentModelSources([...])` or `create` rejects with an unknown-document-type error.

#### Building the actions for a job plan

A job plan carries raw `Action` objects, not reactor calls, so a batch that creates documents needs action creators rather than `reactor.create`. `@powerhousedao/reactor` exports them:

```typescript
import { createDocumentAction, upgradeDocumentAction } from "@powerhousedao/reactor";
import { generateId } from "document-model";

const budgetId = generateId();

const createBudget = createDocumentAction({
  documentId: budgetId,
  model: "powerhouse/document-model", // the document type to create
  version: 0,
});
```

`createDocumentAction(input: CreateDocumentActionInput): Action` returns the `CREATE_DOCUMENT` system action. Put it in the job's `actions` array with `scope: "document"` and `branch: "main"`; the reactor materializes the document when the job runs, so you do not call `reactor.create` first.

#### Registering the built-in models

The document-model model and the drive model ship with the packages. Register them as live modules:

```typescript
import { ReactorBuilder } from "@powerhousedao/reactor";
import { documentModelDocumentModelModule } from "document-model";
import { driveDocumentModelModule, driveCreateDocument, addFile } from "@powerhousedao/shared/document-drive";

const module = await new ReactorBuilder()
  .withDocumentModelSources([documentModelDocumentModelModule, driveDocumentModelModule])
  .buildModule();

// Build a drive PHDocument and hand it to the reactor
const drive = driveCreateDocument();
const job = await module.reactor.create(drive);

// Register a document as a file of that drive
const action = addFile({ id: childId, name: "Budget", documentType: "powerhouse/document-model", parentFolder: null });
```

`driveCreateDocument()` returns an unsaved drive document whose generated id is on `drive.header.id`; `addFile(input)` returns the `ADD_FILE` action to pass in `execute`/`executeBatch` with `scope: "global"`.

const info = await reactor.getJobStatus(jobId);
// info.status is PENDING | RUNNING | WRITE_READY | READ_READY | FAILED

// Or await a terminal status without polling. `InProcessReactorModule` does not
// expose a job awaiter (only `IReactorClient` wires one internally), so build one
// from the module's event bus:
import { JobAwaiter } from "@powerhousedao/reactor";

const awaiter = new JobAwaiter(module.eventBus, (id, signal) =>
  module.reactor.getJobStatus(id, signal),
);
const done = await awaiter.waitForJob(jobId); // resolves at READ_READY or FAILED
awaiter.shutdown(); // rejects anything still pending

```typescript
// Check job status
const info = await reactor.getJobStatus(jobId);
// info.status is PENDING | RUNNING | WRITE_READY | READ_READY | FAILED
```

To await a terminal status instead of polling, construct a `JobAwaiter` over the module's event bus. `buildModule()` does not hand one out — the awaiter is wired inside `IReactorClient`, so on the raw `IReactor` path you create it yourself:

```typescript
import { JobAwaiter } from "@powerhousedao/reactor";

const jobAwaiter = new JobAwaiter(
  reactorModule.eventBus,
  (jobId, signal) => reactorModule.reactor.getJobStatus(jobId, signal),
);

const completed = await jobAwaiter.waitForJob(job.id); // resolves on READ_READY or FAILED

// when you are done, before reactor.kill()
jobAwaiter.shutdown();
```

`waitForJob` subscribes to the job events and falls back to `getJobStatus`, so it is safe against a job that already finished before you called it.

#### Building the actions a batch job submits

A batch job carries `actions`, not a document, so the "create a document" step of a batch is an action rather than `reactor.create()`. `@powerhousedao/reactor` exports the document-lifecycle action creators for this:

```typescript
import { createDocumentAction, upgradeDocumentAction, deleteDocumentAction } from "@powerhousedao/reactor";
import { generateId } from "document-model";

const budgetId = generateId();

const createBudget = createDocumentAction({
  documentId: budgetId,
  model: "powerhouse/document-model",
  version: 0,
});
```

`createDocumentAction(input: CreateDocumentActionInput): Action` emits CREATE_DOCUMENT; `upgradeDocumentAction(input)` sets the initial state; `deleteDocumentAction(documentId)` emits DELETE_DOCUMENT. Use the resulting `Action` values in a job plan's `actions` array.

### Creating a drive on an in-process reactor

The drive document model ships in `@powerhousedao/shared/document-drive`. Register it alongside the document-model module, then create the drive document itself with the `driveCreateDocument` factory and submit it through `reactor.create`:

```typescript
import { ReactorBuilder } from "@powerhousedao/reactor";
import { documentModelDocumentModelModule } from "document-model";
import { driveDocumentModelModule, driveCreateDocument, addFile } from "@powerhousedao/shared/document-drive";

const reactorModule = await new ReactorBuilder()
  .withDocumentModelSources([documentModelDocumentModelModule, driveDocumentModelModule])
  .buildModule();

const driveDoc = driveCreateDocument();      // a fresh DocumentDriveDocument with a generated header.id
const driveJob = await reactorModule.reactor.create(driveDoc);
```

Once the drive exists, `addFile({ id, name, documentType })` builds the ADD_FILE action that registers an already-created document as a file of that drive.

`IEventBus.subscribe` is generic in the payload: `subscribe<K>(type: number, subscriber: (type: number, event: K) => void | Promise<void>): Unsubscribe`. Without the type argument the payload is `unknown` and will not compile under `strict`, so always pass the event type that matches the event id you subscribed to. The reactor job payload types are exported from `@powerhousedao/reactor` as `JobPendingEvent`, `JobRunningEvent`, `JobWriteReadyEvent`, `JobReadReadyEvent` and `ReactorJobFailedEvent`; each carries `jobId` and `jobMeta`.

```typescript
import {
  ReactorEventTypes,
  SyncEventTypes,
  type JobReadReadyEvent,
} from "@powerhousedao/reactor";

// Listen for all completed jobs
const unsubscribe = module.eventBus.subscribe<JobReadReadyEvent>(
  ReactorEventTypes.JOB_READ_READY,
  (type, event) => {
    console.log("Job completed:", event.jobId);
    console.log("Operations:", event.operations.length);
  },
);
```

#### Building the actions for a batch job

Jobs in a batch carry raw `Action` objects, not client calls. To create a new document inside a batch, use the `createDocumentAction` creator exported from `@powerhousedao/reactor`:

```typescript
import { createDocumentAction } from "@powerhousedao/reactor";
import { generateId } from "document-model";

const documentId = generateId();
const action = createDocumentAction({
  documentId,
  model: "powerhouse/document-model",
  version: 0,
  signing: { nonce: generateId() },
});
```

`createDocumentAction(input: CreateDocumentActionInput): Action` emits the CREATE_DOCUMENT action the executor expects. Put it in the job's `actions` array and set the job's `documentId` to the same freshly generated id.

#### Creating a drive at the `IReactor` level

`client.drives.addFile` is the `IReactorClient` path. When you drive `IReactor` directly, build the drive document and the drive actions yourself from `@powerhousedao/shared/document-drive`:

```typescript
import {
  driveDocumentModelModule,
  driveCreateDocument,
  addFile,
} from "@powerhousedao/shared/document-drive";

// register the drive model on the builder
new ReactorBuilder().withDocumentModelSources([driveDocumentModelModule]);

// a new, empty drive document; header.id is the drive id
const drive = driveCreateDocument();
const job = await reactor.create(drive);

// ADD_FILE action for a batch job whose documentId is the drive id
const addFileAction = addFile({
  id: childDocumentId,
  name: "budget",
  documentType: "powerhouse/document-model",
  parentFolder: null,
});
```

Targets a specific branch, scopes or revision when reading documents, and names the identity the read is gated for.

```typescript
type ViewFilter = {
  branch?: string;
  scopes?: string[];
  revision?: number;
  subject?: AuthSubject;
};
```

| Field      | Description                                                                 |
| ---------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| `branch`   | The branch to read from (e.g. `"main"`)                                      |
| `scopes`   | Scopes to include (e.g. `["global"]`)                                        |
| `revision` | Read the document at a specific revision number                             |
| `subject`  | Who the read gate decides for; defaults to the client's own signer. Set it per request when one client serves many principals. `IReactor` ignores it. |

Read on behalf of another identity by naming the subject:

```typescript
const asCarol = await client.get(reportId, { subject: { address: CAROL } });
Object.keys(asCarol.state); // only the scopes Carol's grants allow
```

### The roster document

A `{ group }` principal names a `powerhouse/reactor-group` document. The model ships in `@powerhousedao/reactor-group`; register it with the reactor like any other document model and drive it with its own action creators.

```typescript
import {
  addMember,
  removeMember,
  ReactorGroup,
  utils as groupUtils,
} from "@powerhousedao/reactor-group/document-models/reactor-group";

new ReactorBuilder().withDocumentModelSources([ReactorGroup, myModule]);

const roster = groupUtils.createDocument();
await reactor.create(roster);
await reactor.execute(roster.header.id, "main", [addMember({ address: BOB })]);
await reactor.execute(roster.header.id, "main", [removeMember({ address: BOB })]);
```

The roster id is what a grant's `principal: { group: rosterId }` refers to. Membership changes take effect on the next decision — no write to the governed document's `auth` scope is needed, and its `header.revision.auth` does not move. Group principals only match when the `authGroups` flag is on.

Four actions write the `auth` scope. Their creators are exported from `@powerhousedao/shared/document-model` alongside the `Grant` type:

```typescript
import {
  initializeAuth,
  setGrant,
  removeGrant,
  moveGrant,
  type Grant,
} from "@powerhousedao/shared/document-model";

await reactor.execute(documentId, "main", [
  initializeAuth({ version: 1, grants: myGrants }),
]);
```

`ReactorClientBuilder.buildModule()` returns an `InProcessReactorClientModule`: the `client` plus flat references to the in-process graph — `reactor`, `eventBus`, `documentIndexer`, `documentView`, `signer`, `subscriptionManager` and `jobAwaiter`. It also carries `reactorModule`, the same `InProcessReactorModule` `ReactorBuilder.buildModule()` returns, but that field is optional (`InProcessReactorModule | undefined`) because a client over a remote reactor has no in-process module; narrow it before use. Prefer the flat fields when all you need is the reactor itself. Hand it a `ReactorBuilder` via `withReactorBuilder` and it builds the reactor for you:

```typescript
const module = await new ReactorClientBuilder()
  .withReactorBuilder(builder)
  .buildModule();

const client = module.client;
const reactor = module.reactor; // IReactor, no narrowing needed
```

### Creating a document to pass to `reactor.create`

`IReactor.create` takes a fully-formed `PHDocument`, not a type string — you build it with the creator that ships with the document model. For the built-in drive model:

```typescript
import { ReactorBuilder } from "@powerhousedao/reactor";
import { documentModelDocumentModelModule } from "document-model";
import {
  driveDocumentModelModule,
  driveCreateDocument,
} from "@powerhousedao/shared/document-drive";

const module = await new ReactorBuilder()
  .withDocumentModelSources([
    documentModelDocumentModelModule,
    driveDocumentModelModule,
  ])
  .buildModule();

const drive = driveCreateDocument(); // PHDocument with a fresh header.id
const job = await module.reactor.create(drive);
```

`driveDocumentModelModule` must be registered via `withDocumentModelSources` or the create job fails with an unknown-document-type error. For your own models, the codegen'd package exports the equivalent `<model>CreateDocument` helper. If you would rather not construct the document yourself, use `IReactorClient.createEmpty(documentType)` instead.

| `withJobAwaiter(awaiter)`           | Provide a custom job awaiter                                         |

### Turning a job into a promise with `JobAwaiter`

Every `IReactor` write returns a `PENDING` `JobInfo`. To await completion without polling, construct a `JobAwaiter` over the module's event bus:

```typescript
import { JobAwaiter } from "@powerhousedao/reactor";

const jobAwaiter = new JobAwaiter(module.eventBus, (jobId, signal) =>
  module.reactor.getJobStatus(jobId, signal),
);

const job = await module.reactor.create(document);
const finished = await jobAwaiter.waitForJob(job.id); // resolves on READ_READY or FAILED

jobAwaiter.shutdown(); // unsubscribes and rejects any still-pending waiters
```

`waitForJob` resolves only on the terminal statuses `READ_READY` and `FAILED`; `WRITE_READY` is intermediate. See the warning under [Subscribing to the event bus](#subscribing-to-the-event-bus) before combining `waitForJob` with your own subscribers.

### Creating a document on the `IReactor` path

`IReactor.create` takes an already-built `PHDocument`, not a type string. Every document model module ships a create-document factory next to it; for the built-in drive model both come from `@powerhousedao/shared/document-drive`, and the document-model model itself from `document-model`:

```typescript
import { driveDocumentModelModule, driveCreateDocument } from "@powerhousedao/shared/document-drive";
import { documentModelDocumentModelModule } from "document-model";

const module = await new ReactorBuilder()
  .withDocumentModelSources([documentModelDocumentModelModule, driveDocumentModelModule])
  .buildModule();

const drive = driveCreateDocument(); // type powerhouse/document-drive
const job = await module.reactor.create(drive);
```

A model registered through `withDocumentModelSources` must be in the registry before you create a document of its type, or the job fails.

**Concepts** · `IReactor.executeBatch` · `dependsOn` · `IEventBus.subscribe` · `JobAwaiter.waitForJob` — see [Advanced Reactor Usage](/academy/Reference/Reactor/AdvancedReactorUsage), [Error Handling](/academy/Reference/Reactor/ErrorHandling)

`JobAwaiter` is exported from `@powerhousedao/reactor`. `ReactorBuilder.buildModule()` does not expose one, so construct it from the module's event bus and the reactor's status lookup:

```typescript
import { JobAwaiter } from "@powerhousedao/reactor";

const { reactor, eventBus } = module;
const awaiter = new JobAwaiter(eventBus, (jobId, signal) => reactor.getJobStatus(jobId, signal));
await awaiter.waitForJob(job.id);
awaiter.shutdown(); // rejects anything still pending
```

```typescript
import {
  ReactorEventTypes,
  SyncEventTypes,
  type JobReadReadyEvent,
} from "@powerhousedao/reactor";

// `subscribe<K>` cannot infer the payload: pass it, or the handler sees `unknown`.
const unsubscribe = module.eventBus.subscribe(
  ReactorEventTypes.JOB_READ_READY,
  (type: number, event: JobReadReadyEvent) => {
    console.log("Job completed:", event.jobId);
    console.log("Operations:", event.operations.length);
  },
);
```

Each `ReactorEventTypes` member has a matching exported payload type — `JobPendingEvent`, `JobRunningEvent`, `JobWriteReadyEvent`, `JobReadReadyEvent`, `JobFailedEvent` — all from `@powerhousedao/reactor`.

import type { IReadModel, OperationWithContext } from "@powerhousedao/reactor";

class MyCustomReadModel implements IReadModel {
  // `name` identifies the read model for lookup via getReadModel
  readonly name = "my-custom-read-model";

  async indexOperations(operations: OperationWithContext[]): Promise<void> {
    for (const { operation, context } of operations) {
      // context carries documentId, documentType, scope, branch, ordinal
      // operation carries action, index, timestampUtcMs, hash
    }
  }
}

- **`context`**: `documentId`, `documentType`, `scope`, `branch`, `ordinal` (global ordering), and `resultingState` — a JSON string of the document *after* the operation, shaped as `{ header, ...state }`: the document header under `header`, and every scope's state (`global`, `local`, `auth`, `document`) spread at the top level. There is no `state` wrapper key, so `JSON.parse(resultingState).global` is the global scope state and `JSON.parse(resultingState).header.name` is the document name. Example:

```ts
const parsed = JSON.parse(context.resultingState!) as {
  header: PHDocumentHeader;
  [scope: string]: unknown; // "global", "local", "auth", "document"
};
const title = parsed.header.name ?? "";
const globalState = parsed.global;
```

`DELETE_DOCUMENT` operations carry a reduced payload: only `header` and `document`.

`ReactorClientBuilder.buildModule()` returns both the `client` and the `reactorModule`. Note that `reactorModule` is typed `InProcessReactorModule | undefined` (the field is declared on the host-agnostic `ReactorClientModule`), so under `strict` you must narrow it before use:

```typescript
const { client, reactorModule } = await new ReactorClientBuilder()
  .withReactorBuilder(builder)
  .buildModule();

if (!reactorModule) {
  throw new Error("Expected an in-process reactor module");
}
```

Hand it a `ReactorBuilder` via `withReactorBuilder` and it builds the reactor for you:

### When does the factory actually run?

The processor manager creates processors **per drive**, and it learns about drives from the operation stream: it reacts to a `CREATE_DOCUMENT` whose document type is in the reactor's drive-container set. That set defaults to `DEFAULT_DRIVE_CONTAINER_TYPES` — `"powerhouse/document-drive"` and `"powerhouse/reactor-drive"` — and can be overridden with `ReactorBuilder.withDriveContainerTypes([...])`.

Consequences for standalone scripts and tests:

- Registering a factory against a reactor that has no drive creates no processors and calls no `onOperations`. Create a drive first (`client.drives.create(...)`) and add documents to it with `client.drives.addFile(...)`.
- Registration order does not matter: a factory registered after the drive exists backfills from the drive's history (`startFrom: "beginning"` is the default).

```typescript
const { client, reactorModule } = await new ReactorClientBuilder()
  .withReactorBuilder(new ReactorBuilder().withDocumentModelSources([myModule]))
  .buildModule();

const drive = await client.drives.create({ name: "search-demo" });
await reactorModule!.processorManager.registerFactory("search", async () => [
  { processor: new SearchProcessor(db), filter: { branch: ["main"], documentId: ["*"] } },
]);
await client.drives.addFile(drive.header.id, myDocument);
```

### Bootstrapping a reactor in a Node script

A standalone reactor needs the built-in document models registered explicitly. Both ship with the platform packages:

```ts
import { ReactorBuilder } from "@powerhousedao/reactor";
import {
  documentModelDocumentModelModule,
  documentModelCreateDocument,
} from "document-model";
import {
  driveDocumentModelModule,
  driveCreateDocument,
} from "@powerhousedao/shared/document-drive";

const { reactor, eventBus, processorManager } = await new ReactorBuilder()
  .withDocumentModelSources([
    documentModelDocumentModelModule, // the document-model model itself
    driveDocumentModelModule,         // required to create drives
  ])
  .buildModule();

// `driveCreateDocument()` / `documentModelCreateDocument()` build the initial
// PHDocument to hand to `reactor.create(document)`.
```

### Reacting to document deletion

A deletion reaches a processor as an ordinary operation whose `operation.action.type` is `"DELETE_DOCUMENT"`. Its input is `{ documentId: string; propagate?: "none" | "cascade" }`; `documentId` names the document that was deleted, which is what you should key your cleanup on, falling back to `context.documentId` when the input is absent. Neither `DeleteDocumentAction` nor `DeleteDocumentActionInput` is part of the public export surface of `@powerhousedao/reactor` or `document-model`, so narrow `action.input` yourself:

```typescript
if (operation.action.type === "DELETE_DOCUMENT") {
  const input = (operation.action.input ?? {}) as { documentId?: string };
  await this.removeRow(input.documentId ?? context.documentId);
}
```

#### Creating a document inside a batch

`IReactorClient.createEmpty` and `client.drives.addFile` are not available on `IReactor`. To create a document as one job of an `executeBatch`, build the genesis action pair yourself with the action creators exported from `@powerhousedao/reactor`:

```typescript
import { createDocumentAction, upgradeDocumentAction } from "@powerhousedao/reactor";
import { documentModelCreateDocument } from "document-model";

const doc = documentModelCreateDocument();

const actions = [
  createDocumentAction({
    model: doc.header.documentType, // e.g. "powerhouse/document-model"
    version: 0,                     // always 0: the pre-upgrade genesis version
    documentId: doc.header.id,
    signing: {
      signature: doc.header.id,
      publicKey: doc.header.sig.publicKey,
      nonce: doc.header.sig.nonce,
      createdAtUtcIso: doc.header.createdAtUtcIso,
      documentType: doc.header.documentType,
    },
    slug: doc.header.slug,
    name: doc.header.name,
    branch: doc.header.branch,
    meta: doc.header.meta,
    protocolVersions: doc.header.protocolVersions ?? { "base-reducer": 2 },
  }),
  upgradeDocumentAction({
    documentId: doc.header.id,
    model: doc.header.documentType,
    fromVersion: 0,
    toVersion: 1, // the document-model version the initial state conforms to
    initialState: doc.state,
  }),
];
```

Both actions are document-scope, so the job plan that carries them must use `scope: "document"`.

const job = await reactor.create(document, signer);
```

