`waitForJob` resolves with the terminal `JobInfo`, for `FAILED` as well as `READ_READY`. It reads the current status first, so a job that finished before the call resolves immediately. Resolving means the job reached a terminal status. It does not mean every other `JOB_READ_READY` subscriber has run: subscribers run sequentially in registration order, and the awaiter registers its own subscriber when it is constructed. So a subscriber registered *after* the awaiter may still be pending when `waitForJob` returns — if your continuation then calls `reactor.kill()` or `process.exit()`, that subscriber never runs and you see no output and no error. Two fixes: register your own subscriber before constructing the `JobAwaiter`, so it runs first in the emit order; or resolve a promise from inside your subscriber and await that promise as well as `waitForJob`.

```typescript
let ready: () => void;
const handlerDone = new Promise<void>((resolve) => (ready = resolve));

const unsubscribe = eventBus.subscribe(
  ReactorEventTypes.JOB_READ_READY,
  (_type, event: JobReadReadyEvent) => {
    console.log("read models are current for", event.jobId);
    ready();
  },
);

const awaiter = new JobAwaiter(eventBus, (jobId, signal) =>
  reactor.getJobStatus(jobId, signal),
);

const job = await reactor.create(document);
await awaiter.waitForJob(job.id);
await handlerDone;

unsubscribe();
awaiter.shutdown();
```

### Reacting to document deletion

`DELETE_DOCUMENT` is a platform action on the `document` scope, created by `deleteDocumentAction(documentId)` from `@powerhousedao/reactor`. Its `input` is `{ documentId: string }`. A processor that maintains a row per document should therefore resolve the deleted id the same way the reactor does — from the action input, falling back to the operation context:

```typescript
if (operation.action.type === "DELETE_DOCUMENT") {
  const input = operation.action.input as { documentId?: string } | undefined;
  const documentId = input?.documentId ?? context.documentId;
  await db.deleteFrom("my_table").where("doc_id", "=", documentId).execute();
  continue;
}
```

### `create`

Create a new drive document and wait for completion.

```typescript
create(
  input: DriveInput,
  signal?: AbortSignal,
): Promise<DocumentDriveDocument>
```

`DriveInput` comes from `@powerhousedao/shared/document-drive`:

```typescript
type DriveInput = {
  global: { name: string; icon?: string | null };
  id?: string;
  slug?: string;
  preferredEditor?: string;
  local?: Partial<DocumentDriveLocalState>;
};
```

Only `global.name` is required; `id` is generated when omitted.

```typescript
const drive = await client.drives.create({ global: { name: "my-drive" } });
```

| `document`        | `PHDocument`  | Yes      | The document to create. Build it with the model's own `createDocument()` (for example `documentModelCreateDocument()`), then set `document.header.name` before calling `addFile` — that name is passed through on the `CREATE_DOCUMENT` action and becomes both the document's `header.name` and the drive node's name. |

```typescript
import { documentModelCreateDocument } from "document-model";

const document = documentModelCreateDocument();
document.header.name = "Quarterly Budget Report";
await client.drives.addFile(drive.header.id, document);
```

`onOperations` is called post-ready with the operations that matched this processor's filter, during both live routing and backfill. Each `OperationWithContext` is `{ operation, context }`; `context` carries `documentId`, `documentType`, `scope`, `branch`, the global `ordinal`, and optionally `resultingState`.

Check `operation.action.type` before you trust `context.resultingState`. The `document`-scope lifecycle actions (`CREATE_DOCUMENT`, `UPGRADE_DOCUMENT`, `DELETE_DOCUMENT`) are delivered like any other operation, but a `DELETE_DOCUMENT` record carries no usable `resultingState` and names the document it removes in `operation.action.input.documentId` — fall back to `context.documentId` only when the input omits it. A processor that maintains rows keyed by document must handle deletes on that branch first and skip the state-parsing path:

```typescript
if (operation.action.type === "DELETE_DOCUMENT") {
  const { documentId } = operation.action.input as { documentId?: string };
  await removeRow(documentId ?? context.documentId);
  continue;
}
if (!context.resultingState) continue;
```

##### Register the processor

The processor is a plain `IProcessor`, so it is registered through the reactor module's processor manager. The factory is called once per drive, so a drive must already exist (or be created afterwards) before `onOperations` fires:

```ts
import { SearchProcessor } from "@powerhousedao/example-full-text-search";
import type { ProcessorFilter, ProcessorRecord } from "@powerhousedao/reactor";

const filter: ProcessorFilter = {
  branch: ["main"],
  documentId: ["*"],
  scope: ["global"],
};

await reactorModule.processorManager.registerFactory(
  "@powerhousedao/example-full-text-search",
  (): ProcessorRecord[] => [
    { processor: new SearchProcessor(db), filter, startFrom: "beginning" },
  ],
);
```

`startFrom: "beginning"` (the default) back-indexes the drive's existing history. See [Processors](/academy/Reference/Reactor/Processors) for the factory contract and catch-up rules.

- PostgreSQL with full-text search support (`tsvector`, `plainto_tsquery`, `ts_rank`) — either a real server, or [PGlite](https://pglite.dev/) in-process, which is what the recipe's own tests and `demo.ts` use
- A [Kysely](https://kysely.dev/) instance typed with `SearchDB`

To run entirely in-process (no server, works in CI), build the Kysely instance on PGlite. `KyselyPGlite` takes no arguments for plain full-text search — the `{ extensions: … }` option is only needed for Postgres extensions such as pgvector — and exposes the dialect Kysely needs:

```ts
import { Kysely } from "kysely";
import { KyselyPGlite } from "kysely-pglite";
import { up, type SearchDB } from "@powerhousedao/example-full-text-search";

const pglite = new KyselyPGlite();
const db = new Kysely<SearchDB>({ dialect: pglite.dialect });
await up(db);
```

In a `ph` project your processor factory is registered automatically when it is included in your project's processor exports. After creating the files above, make sure they are exported from your `processors/index.ts`:

```typescript
export { operationLoggerProcessorFactory } from "./operation-logger/factory.js";
```

Outside a `ph` project — a standalone Node script, CLI tool, or test — register the factory yourself on the reactor module's processor manager. `buildModule()` (not `build()`) returns the in-process module that exposes it:

```typescript
import { ReactorBuilder } from "@powerhousedao/reactor";

const { reactor, processorManager } = await new ReactorBuilder()
  .withDocumentModelSources([documentModelDocumentModelModule, driveDocumentModelModule])
  .buildModule();

await processorManager.registerFactory("operation-logger", () => [
  { processor: new OperationLoggerProcessor("standalone"), filter, startFrom: "beginning" },
]);
```

The factory is only invoked once a drive exists, so create a drive before you expect `onOperations` to fire. See [Processors](/academy/Reference/Reactor/Processors) for the full `IProcessorManager` surface and [Advanced reactor usage](/academy/Reference/Reactor/AdvancedReactorUsage) for `buildModule()`.

Each creator takes an optional initial state and falls back to the model's defaults. Neither the creators nor `reactor.create` take a document name: `CreateDocumentOptions` carries only `parentIdentifier` and `documentModelVersion`, and `rename()` exists on `IReactorClient`, not on `IReactor`. On the `IReactor` path, set the name on the header of the freshly created document before submitting it:

```typescript
const todoList = todoListModule.utils.createDocument();
todoList.header.name = "Q3 Roadmap";
const todoJob = await reactor.create(todoList, signer);
```

The header travels with the `CREATE_DOCUMENT` job, so the name is present the first time any processor sees the document in `context.resultingState`.

import { useState, type FormEventHandler } from "react";
