### A local signer for scripts and tests

`RenownCryptoSigner` needs a Renown account and the `@renown/sdk` dependency. A standalone Node script, a test, or a demo can implement `ISigner` directly on top of Web Crypto — the interface is small and nothing else in the stack is required:

```typescript
import type { Action, ISigner, Signature } from "document-model";

export async function createLocalSigner(address: string): Promise<ISigner> {
  const { publicKey, privateKey } = (await crypto.subtle.generateKey(
    { name: "ECDSA", namedCurve: "P-256" },
    true,
    ["sign", "verify"],
  )) as CryptoKeyPair;

  const signRaw = async (data: Uint8Array) =>
    new Uint8Array(
      await crypto.subtle.sign(
        { name: "ECDSA", hash: "SHA-256" },
        privateKey,
        // re-wrap: Web Crypto's BufferSource is invariant over ArrayBuffer under `strict`
        new Uint8Array(data),
      ),
    );

  return {
    user: { address, networkId: "eip155", chainId: 1 },
    app: { name: "local-script", key: `did:key:${address}` },
    publicKey,
    sign: signRaw,
    async verify(data, signature) {
      const ok = await crypto.subtle.verify(
        { name: "ECDSA", hash: "SHA-256" },
        publicKey,
        new Uint8Array(signature),
        new Uint8Array(data),
      );
      if (!ok) throw new Error("invalid signature");
    },
    async signAction(action: Action): Promise<Signature> {
      const bytes = await signRaw(new TextEncoder().encode(JSON.stringify(action)));
      const hex = `0x${Buffer.from(bytes).toString("hex")}`;
      return [String(Math.floor(Date.now() / 1000)), `did:key:${address}`, "", "", hex];
    },
  };
}
```

The auth policy matches `{ address }` principals against `signer.user.address`, so that field is what decides which grants apply. Pass the result to `ReactorClientBuilder.withSigner()`. To act as a second identity from the same process, attach `action.context.signer` yourself — the client passes through any action that already carries signatures.

A `{ group }` principal names a roster document (`powerhouse/reactor-group`) and resolves live, so hiring and offboarding are single membership operations on the roster rather than edits to every policy that trusts it. The roster model ships in `@powerhousedao/reactor-group`: the `ReactorGroupV1` module is exported from `@powerhousedao/reactor-group/document-models`, the ready-made `documentModels` array and `upgradeManifests` from the package root, and `reactorGroupDocumentType` from `@powerhousedao/reactor-group/document-models/reactor-group`. Register the module with `withDocumentModelSources`, create the roster from its own utils, and edit membership with its own creators:

```typescript
import { ReactorGroupV1 } from "@powerhousedao/reactor-group/document-models";

new ReactorBuilder().withDocumentModelSources([ReactorGroupV1 /* , ... */ ]);

const roster = await client.create(
  ReactorGroupV1.utils.createDocument({
    global: { name: "Reviewers", description: "Expense reviewers", members: [] },
  }),
);

await client.execute(roster.header.id, "main", [
  ReactorGroupV1.actions.addMember({ address: bobAddress }),
]);
await client.execute(roster.header.id, "main", [
  ReactorGroupV1.actions.removeMember({ address: bobAddress }),
]);
```

The roster's membership lives in its `global` scope (`{ name, description, members }`) on its `main` branch, and is governed by the roster's own auth policy. A `where` clause reads `subject.*`, `doc.<scope>.*` and `action.input.*`, which is how a grant can depend on the values of the operation it gates.

A `{ group }` principal names a roster document (`powerhouse/reactor-group`) and resolves live, so hiring and offboarding are single membership operations on the roster rather than edits to every policy that trusts it. The roster model ships in `@powerhousedao/reactor-group`. Register the module with `withDocumentModelSources` — it is exported as `ReactorGroupV1` from `@powerhousedao/reactor-group/document-models`, and as `ReactorGroup` from `@powerhousedao/reactor-group/document-models/reactor-group`, which also exports `utils` (roster documents), the membership creators `addMember({ address })` and `removeMember({ address })`, and `reactorGroupDocumentType`:

