`RenownCryptoSigner` is the standard production implementation. It derives signing keys from the Renown identity system and identifies signers using DID URIs (`did:key:z...`).

### A minimal signer

Outside the browser — a script, a test, or a demo that plays several identities from one process — implement `ISigner` directly. Nothing in the reactor verifies the signature bytes unless you also pass a `verifier`, so the fields that matter for authorization are `user.address` (matched by `{ address }` grants) and `app.key` (the `did:key` seen as `subject.key`).

```typescript
import type { Action, ISigner, Signature } from "document-model";

export async function createSigner(address: string): Promise<ISigner> {
  const keyPair = await crypto.subtle.generateKey(
    { name: "ECDSA", namedCurve: "P-256" },
    true,
    ["sign", "verify"],
  );
  return {
    user: { address, networkId: "eip155", chainId: 1 },
    app: { name: "my-script", key: `did:key:${address}` },
    publicKey: keyPair.publicKey,
    async sign(data: Uint8Array) {
      const sig = await crypto.subtle.sign(
        { name: "ECDSA", hash: "SHA-256" },
        keyPair.privateKey,
        data as BufferSource,
      );
      return new Uint8Array(sig);
    },
    async verify(data: Uint8Array, signature: Uint8Array) {
      const ok = await crypto.subtle.verify(
        { name: "ECDSA", hash: "SHA-256" },
        keyPair.publicKey,
        signature as BufferSource,
        data as BufferSource,
      );
      if (!ok) throw new Error("invalid signature");
    },
    async signAction(action: Action): Promise<Signature> {
      const sig = await this.sign(new TextEncoder().encode(action.id));
      return [
        String(Math.floor(Date.now() / 1000)),
        `did:key:${address}`,
        "",
        "",
        `0x${Buffer.from(sig).toString("hex")}`,
      ];
    },
  };
}
```

`Uint8Array` must be widened to `BufferSource` for the Web Crypto calls under `@types/node` 24.

To run several per-identity clients over one already-built reactor, pass each the same gate through `withReadGate`. `ModelReadGate`, `BareReadGate` and `readDecisionModel` are exported from `@powerhousedao/reactor`. Build the gate from the module you already hold:

```typescript
import { BareReadGate, ModelReadGate, readDecisionModel } from "@powerhousedao/reactor";

const model = readDecisionModel(module.featureFlags, module.registry);
const gate = model
  ? new ModelReadGate(model, module.documentView, module.featureFlags.authGroups)
  : new BareReadGate();

const aliceClient = await new ReactorClientBuilder()
  .withReactor(module.reactor, module.eventBus, module.documentIndexer, module.documentView)
  .withSigner(aliceSigner)
  .withReadGate(gate)
  .build();
```

`readDecisionModel` returns `undefined` below `authEnforcement`, where there is no model to enforce; fall back to `BareReadGate` there. The third argument, `servesGroups`, decides whether a group a policy names is served to that policy's audience — pass the reactor's `authGroups` flag.

If you only need one client to answer for many principals, prefer `ViewFilter.subject` per request; it needs none of the above.

#### The shape of a gated read

```typescript
import { ReactorBuilder, ReactorClientBuilder } from "@powerhousedao/reactor";

const builder = new ReactorBuilder()
  .withDocumentModelSources([ExpenseReport, ReactorGroupV1, documentModelDocumentModelModule])
  .withExecutorConfig({
    featureFlags: { documentDecisions: true, authEnforcement: true, authGroups: true },
  });

const { client, reactorModule } = await new ReactorClientBuilder()
  .withReactorBuilder(builder)
  .withSigner(aliceSigner)
  .buildModule();

// Inside the trust boundary: every scope, for anybody.
const raw = await reactorModule.reactor.get(reportId);
Object.keys(raw.state); // ["auth", "document", "global", "local"]

// Through the client, as the client's own signer (Alice).
const mine = await client.get(reportId);
Object.keys(mine.state); // ["auth", "document", "global"]

// One client, many principals: name the subject per request.
const asMallory = await client.get(reportId, { subject: { address: "mallory" } });
Object.keys(asMallory.state); // ["auth", "document"] — withheld scopes are absent, not empty
```

#### Gotchas worth knowing

- **One scope per `execute`** — all actions in one call must share a scope, so an `auth`-scope policy write cannot be batched with a `global`-scope domain action.
- **The auth stream is strictly timestamp-monotonic** — two policy writes in the same millisecond are refused (`AuthTimestampNotMonotonicError`). Space them.
- **Client-created documents are unsigned** — `client.createEmpty()` produces a header with an empty `sig.publicKey` even with `.withSigner()`, so there is no creator carve-out and the initial policy must itself keep someone able to administer `auth`.

const job = await reactor.execute(docId, "main", actions);
```

`execute` and `load` take no signer: their signature is `execute(docId, branch, actions, signal?, meta?)`. The identity a write is attributed to rides on each action, in `action.context.signer`. With `authEnforcement` on, an action submitted through `IReactor.execute` without that context is decided as an anonymous subject, no `{ address }` grant matches and the job fails. `IReactorClient.execute` fills it in from the client's `ISigner`; a caller driving `IReactor` directly attaches it:

```typescript
const signed = {
  ...action,
  context: {
    ...action.context,
    signer: {
      user: { address: aliceAddress, networkId: "eip155", chainId: 1 },
      app: { name: "my-script", key: "did:key:z..." },
      signatures: [],
    },
  },
};
const job = await reactor.execute(docId, "main", [signed]);
```

An empty `signatures` array is accepted unless a `SignatureVerificationHandler` is configured; it names the subject for the policy without proving it.

```typescript

```typescript
// Execute actions. Note: `execute` takes no signer.
const job = await reactor.execute(docId, "main", actions);
```

Unlike `create`, `deleteDocument` and `addRelationship`, `execute` and `load` accept no `ISigner`. On the `IReactor` path the acting identity of a write is read from each action's own `context.signer`, so a caller that submits on behalf of several identities attaches it per action:

```typescript
function signedBy<A extends Action>(action: A, address: string): A {
  return {
    ...action,
    context: {
      ...action.context,
      signer: {
        user: { address, networkId: "eip155", chainId: 1 },
        app: { name: "my-app", key: `did:demo:${address}` },
        signatures: [],
      },
    },
  };
}

await reactor.execute(docId, "main", [signedBy(submitExpense(input), aliceAddress)]);
```

This is what the auth policy matches an `{ address }` principal against. An `IReactorClient` does the same thing for you from its configured `withSigner(...)`; see [Signing](/academy/Build/BuildingUserExperiences/Authorization/Signing).

`applyUpgradeDocumentAction` is typed `(document: PHDocument, action: UpgradeDocumentAction, upgradePath?: UpgradeTransition[]) => PHDocument`: the state type is erased on both sides, because a manifest's transitions are `UpgradeReducer<any, any>` and a manifest can span many versions. The returned document really carries the target version's state, so re-type it at the call site, and make any wrapper you write generic in the target state rather than returning a bare `PHDocument`:

```typescript
import type { PHBaseState, PHDocument, UpgradeManifest } from "document-model";
import type { TodoPHState as StateV2 } from "document-models/todo/v2";

export function upgradeDocument<TTo extends PHBaseState>(
  document: PHDocument,
  manifest: UpgradeManifest<readonly number[]>,
): PHDocument<TTo> {
  // ...build the action and the path as above...
  return applyUpgradeDocumentAction(document, action, path) as PHDocument<TTo>;
}

const upgraded = upgradeDocument<StateV2>(document, todoUpgradeManifest);
```

Without the explicit target parameter the result is `PHDocument<PHBaseState>`, so `upgraded.state.global` does not typecheck and the v2 `Reducer<TodoPHState>` cannot be passed to anything inferred from it.

// The ordered transitions for the range (from+1)..to. Unlike the registry's
// computeUpgradePath, this standalone helper throws plain `Error`s, not the
// typed registry errors: `No upgrade manifest provided for transition from
// version X to Y` when `manifest` is undefined, and `Upgrade manifest for
// "<type>" is missing step "vN" (from vX to vY). Available keys: ...` when a
// hop is absent. It does not check `fromVersion` against `supportedVersions`
// and it has no "up to latest" mode — pass `toVersion` explicitly.

Replay never re-runs an upgrade — but `replayDocument` does not skip anything for you. It replays every scope present in the `operations` map you pass, so strip the non-domain scopes (`document` and `auth`) yourself; the upgrade survives only through the migrated `initialState`.

```typescript
import { replayDocument } from "document-model";

const NON_DOMAIN_SCOPES = new Set(["document", "auth"]);

const domainOps = Object.fromEntries(
  Object.entries(document.operations).filter(
    ([scope]) => !NON_DOMAIN_SCOPES.has(scope),
  ),
);

const rebuilt = replayDocument(
  document.initialState,
  domainOps,
  reducer,          // the *latest* version's reducer
  document.header,
  undefined,        // dispatch
  undefined,        // skipHeaderOperations
  { checkHashes: false },
);
```

Full signature: `replayDocument(initialState, operations, reducer, header, dispatch?, skipHeaderOperations?, options?)`. `options` is `ReplayDocumentOptions` = `{ checkHashes?, reuseOperationResultingState?, operationResultingStateParser?, skipIndexValidation? }`. Pass `{ checkHashes: false }` when replaying a log recorded under an older version: the per-operation hashes were computed over the old state shape. The final hash of each scope is still verified, and a replay that diverges throws `HashMismatchError` (`Hash mismatch on document …, scope global`).

Replay never re-runs an upgrade — but only because the caller does not hand it the upgrade. `replayDocument(initialState, operations, reducer, header, dispatch?, skipHeaderOperations?, options?)` replays every scope present in the `operations` you pass, so pass only the **domain** scopes. The non-domain scopes are `"document"` (where `UPGRADE_DOCUMENT` and `DELETE_DOCUMENT` are recorded) and `"auth"`; everything else (`"global"`, `"local"`, and any model-specific scope) is a domain scope and must be replayed. This is what `baseLoadFromInput` does when the reactor loads a document:

```typescript
const NON_DOMAIN_SCOPES = new Set(["document", "auth"]);
const domainOps = Object.fromEntries(
  Object.entries(document.operations).filter(([scope]) => !NON_DOMAIN_SCOPES.has(scope)),
);
const rebuilt = replayDocument(
  document.initialState,
  domainOps,
  reducer,
  document.header,
  undefined,
  undefined,
  { checkHashes: false },
);
```

When replaying a log recorded under an older version through the current reducer, pass `{ checkHashes: false }` in `options`. The per-operation hashes were computed over the old state shape, so only the final hash of each scope is checked; a mismatch throws `HashMismatchError`, whose message embeds `Hash mismatch on document <id>, scope <scope>, index <n>`.

Codegen emits every field. If you write a module by hand, build `reducer` with `createReducer(stateReducer)` from `document-model`. `createReducer` wraps a `StateReducer<TState>` (`(state: Draft<TState>, action, dispatch?) => TState | undefined`) in the base document reducer, so `SET_NAME`, `UNDO`, `REDO` and `PRUNE` keep working. A state reducer that mutates the draft in place must still be annotated to return `TState | undefined`; a body whose branches all use a bare `return;` infers `void` and will not assign to `StateReducer<TState>`.

`utils` is a `DocumentModelUtils<TState>`. Codegen assembles it in `gen/utils.ts`, but the whole object is small enough to write by hand:

```typescript
import {
  baseCreateDocument,
  baseLoadFromInput,
  baseSaveToFileHandle,
  createBaseState,
  type DocumentModelUtils,
} from "document-model";

const createState = (state?: Partial<TodoPHState>): TodoPHState => ({
  ...createBaseState(state?.auth, state?.document),
  global: { title: "", todos: [], ...state?.global },
  local: {},
});

export const utils: DocumentModelUtils<TodoPHState> = {
  fileExtension: "todo",
  createState,
  createDocument: (initialState) =>
    baseCreateDocument<TodoPHState>(createState, initialState, TODO_DOCUMENT_TYPE),
  loadFromInput: (input) => baseLoadFromInput<TodoPHState>(input, reducer),
  saveToFileHandle: baseSaveToFileHandle,
  isStateOfType: (state): state is TodoPHState => true,
  assertIsStateOfType: () => {},
  isDocumentOfType: (doc): doc is PHDocument<TodoPHState> =>
    (doc as PHDocument).header?.documentType === TODO_DOCUMENT_TYPE,
  assertIsDocumentOfType: () => {},
};
```

`documentModel` is a full `DocumentModelPHState`. Only `global.id` (the document type key) and `global.name` are read by the registry, but the object must be complete. Build it with `createState(defaultBaseState(), { ... })` from `document-model`:

```typescript
import { createState, defaultBaseState } from "document-model";

export const documentModel = createState(defaultBaseState(), {
  global: {
    id: TODO_DOCUMENT_TYPE,          // the registry key
    name: "Todo",
    extension: "todo",
    description: "",
    author: { name: "", website: null },
    specifications: [],              // may be empty for a hand-written module
  },
  local: {},
});
```

Wiring at startup is two stages: build the host module, call the builder to get a factory, then register it.

```typescript
const factory = await todoIndexerFactoryBuilder(processorHostModule);
await reactorModule.processorManager.registerFactory("@my-org/my-package", factory);
```

**Where `processorHostModule` comes from.** Connect and Switchboard construct the host module for you and pass it to every package's factory builder. In a standalone reactor (a Node script or test that builds its own `ReactorBuilder` / `ReactorClientBuilder`) there is no host, so you assemble the module yourself from the built `ReactorModule` and register the factory directly:

```typescript
const { client, reactorModule } = await new ReactorClientBuilder()
  .withReactorBuilder(builder)
  .buildModule();

const dispatch: IProcessorDispatch = {
  execute: (documentId, branch, actions, signal, meta) =>
    client.executeAsync(documentId, branch, actions, signal, meta),
};

await reactorModule.processorManager.registerFactory("my-jobs", () => [
  { processor: new MyProcessor(dispatch), filter },
]);
```

`IProcessorDispatch` is exported from `@powerhousedao/shared`, not from `@powerhousedao/reactor`. Building it over `client.executeAsync` keeps the no-blocking rule below intact: the call returns a `JobInfo` immediately instead of waiting for `READ_READY`. Never hand a processor the `IReactorClient` itself for writes - see [Processor best practices](/academy/Build/WorkWithData/ProcessorBestPractices).

Codegen emits every field. If you write a module by hand, build `reducer` with `createReducer(stateReducer)` from `document-model`. `createReducer` wraps a `StateReducer<TState>` (`(state: Draft<TState>, action, dispatch?) => TState | undefined`) in the base document reducer, so `SET_NAME`, `UNDO`, `REDO` and `PRUNE` keep working. `utils` is a `DocumentModelUtils<TState>`, which a hand-written module can assemble directly:

```typescript
import {
  baseCreateDocument,
  baseLoadFromInput,
  baseSaveToFileHandle,
  createBaseState,
  type CreateState,
  type DocumentModelUtils,
  type PHBaseState,
  type PHDocument,
} from "document-model";

type DocumentModelUtils<TState extends PHBaseState> = {
  fileExtension: string;
  createState: CreateState<TState>;                 // (state?: TState | Partial<TState>) => TState
  createDocument: (initialState?: Partial<TState>, createState?: CreateState<TState>) => PHDocument<TState>;
  loadFromInput: (input: FileInput) => PHDocument<TState> | Promise<PHDocument<TState>>;
  saveToFileHandle: (document: PHDocument, input: FileSystemFileHandle) => void | Promise<void>;
  isStateOfType: (state: unknown) => state is TState;
  assertIsStateOfType: (state: unknown) => asserts state is TState;
  isDocumentOfType: (document: unknown) => document is PHDocument<TState>;
  assertIsDocumentOfType: (document: unknown) => asserts document is PHDocument<TState>;
};
```

`FileInput` is `string | number[] | Uint8Array | ArrayBuffer | Blob`. Build the pieces with the base helpers:

```typescript
const createState: CreateState<MyPHState> = (state) => ({
  ...createBaseState(state?.auth, state?.document),
  global: { ...defaultGlobal, ...state?.global },
  local: {},
});

const createDocument = (initialState?: Partial<MyPHState>) =>
  baseCreateDocument<MyPHState>(createState, initialState, "my-org/my-type");

const loadFromInput = (input: FileInput) => baseLoadFromInput<MyPHState>(input, myReducer);
const saveToFileHandle = baseSaveToFileHandle;
```

`documentModel.global.id` must equal the document type string you pass to `baseCreateDocument`; `documentModel` itself is a `DocumentModelPHState`, i.e. `{ ...createBaseState(), global: { author: { name, website }, description, extension, id, name, specifications: [] }, local: {} }`.

Wiring at startup is two stages: build the host module, call the builder to get a factory, then register it. Connect and Switchboard build the host module for you and pass it to every exported factory builder. In a standalone reactor you own that object — supply the fields your processors actually read and register the resulting factory yourself:

```typescript
const { client, reactorModule } = await new ReactorClientBuilder()
  .withReactorBuilder(reactorBuilder)
  .buildModule();

const processorHostModule = {
  client,
  processorApp: "switchboard" as const,
  dispatch: {
    execute: (docId, branch, actions, signal, meta) =>
      client.executeAsync(docId, branch, actions, { signal, meta }),
  },
  // analyticsStore / relationalDb / getReadModel only if your processors use them
} as IProcessorHostModule;

const factory = await todoIndexerFactoryBuilder(processorHostModule);
await reactorModule.processorManager.registerFactory("@my-org/my-package", factory);
```

A plain `IProcessor` that needs nothing from the host can skip the builder and be registered as a bare `ProcessorFactory`, but it must then use `client.executeAsync` for write-backs — see [Processor best practices](/academy/Build/WorkWithData/ProcessorBestPractices).

Codegen emits every field. If you write a module by hand, build `reducer` with `createReducer(stateReducer)` from `document-model`. `createReducer` wraps a `StateReducer<TState>` (`(state: Draft<TState>, action, dispatch?) => TState | undefined`) in the base document reducer, so `SET_NAME`, `UNDO`, `REDO` and `PRUNE` keep working. `utils` is a `DocumentModelUtils<TState>`; the whole object is nine fields, and a hand-written one looks like this:

```typescript
import {
  baseCreateDocument,
  baseLoadFromInput,
  baseSaveToFileHandle,
  createBaseState,
  createReducer,
  type DocumentModelUtils,
} from "document-model";

const DOCUMENT_TYPE = "powerhouse/deployment";

const createState = (state?: Partial<DeploymentPHState>): DeploymentPHState => ({
  ...createBaseState(state?.auth, state?.document),
  global: { name: "", spec: "", status: "DRAFT", result: null, ...state?.global },
  local: {},
});

const reducer = createReducer<DeploymentPHState>(stateReducer);

const utils: DocumentModelUtils<DeploymentPHState> = {
  fileExtension: "phdm",
  createState,
  createDocument: (initialState) =>
    baseCreateDocument<DeploymentPHState>(createState, initialState, DOCUMENT_TYPE),
  loadFromInput: (input) => baseLoadFromInput<DeploymentPHState>(input, reducer),
  saveToFileHandle: baseSaveToFileHandle,
  isStateOfType: (state): state is DeploymentPHState => typeof state === "object",
  assertIsStateOfType: (state) => state as DeploymentPHState,
  isDocumentOfType: (doc) => doc.header.documentType === DOCUMENT_TYPE,
  assertIsDocumentOfType: (doc) => doc as DeploymentDocument,
};
```

```typescript
import { JobAwaiter, ReactorBuilder, ReactorEventTypes, type JobReadReadyEvent } from "@powerhousedao/reactor";

const { reactor, eventBus } = await new ReactorBuilder()
  .withDocumentModelSources([todoListModule])
  .buildModule();

const awaiter = new JobAwaiter(eventBus, (jobId, signal) =>
  reactor.getJobStatus(jobId, signal),
);

const job = await reactor.create(document);
const info = await awaiter.waitForJob(job.id); // READ_READY or FAILED, so check info.status

awaiter.shutdown(); // rejects anything still pending
```

`waitForJob` resolves with the terminal `JobInfo`, for `FAILED` as well as `READ_READY`. It reads the current status first, so a job that finished before the call resolves immediately.

:::warning `waitForJob` does not wait for your own subscriber
Resolving means the job reached a terminal status. Subscribers run sequentially in registration order, and the awaiter registers its own subscriber when it is constructed, so the continuation after `await waitForJob(...)` can run before your `JOB_READ_READY` handler does. If you then call `process.exit()` or `reactor.kill()`, your handler never runs and you see no output at all. When your continuation depends on your own subscriber's work, await a promise the subscriber resolves:

```typescript
let onHandled: () => void;
const handled = new Promise<void>((resolve) => { onHandled = resolve; });

const unsubscribe = eventBus.subscribe(
  ReactorEventTypes.JOB_READ_READY,
  (_type, event: JobReadReadyEvent) => {
    console.log("read-ready", event.jobId);
    onHandled();
  },
);

const job = await reactor.create(document);
await handled; // not just awaiter.waitForJob(job.id)

unsubscribe();
reactor.kill();
```
:::

Read models registered via `withReadModel()` run in the pre-ready phase — they complete before `JOB_READ_READY` fires. This is in contrast to processors (registered via `ProcessorManager`), which run in the post-ready phase.

To observe that guarantee, build with `buildModule()` (not `build()`) so you get the event bus, and read your model from inside a `JOB_READ_READY` subscriber:

```typescript
import { ReactorBuilder, ReactorEventTypes, type JobReadReadyEvent } from "@powerhousedao/reactor";

const { reactor, eventBus } = await new ReactorBuilder()
  .withDocumentModelSources([todoListModule])
  .withReadModel(myReadModel)
  .buildModule();

eventBus.subscribe(
  ReactorEventTypes.JOB_READ_READY,
  (_type, event: JobReadReadyEvent) => {
    // myReadModel.indexOperations has already run for event.operations
  },
);
```

If your script has to wait for that subscriber before shutting down, see [Awaiting a job with `JobAwaiter`](#awaiting-a-job-with-jobawaiter): `waitForJob` resolving does not mean your subscriber has run.

Each `OperationWithContext` is `{ operation, context }`. `context` carries `documentId`, `documentType`, `scope`, `branch`, the global `ordinal`, and optionally `resultingState` — the same shape processors receive in `onOperations`. Key your derived view off `context.documentType` (the document model's type string, e.g. `powerhouse/document-drive`) or `context.documentId`.

```typescript
async indexOperations(operations: OperationWithContext[]): Promise<void> {
  for (const { context } of operations) {
    const type = context.documentType; // e.g. "powerhouse/document-drive"
    // Build your derived view
  }
}
```
